<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Yue</h1><p class="st-meta">510 BCE – 306 BCE (approx.) · capital Kuaiji</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Goujian, who endured humiliation, rebuilt his state and destroyed Wu in 473 BCE, a story of patient revenge known to every Chinese reader.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Yue, or Yuyue, was a state of the Yue peoples around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaoxing">Kuaiji</a> (Shaoxing) in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>. Its rulers claimed descent from a son of a Xia king sent to tend the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>. Yue appears in the record from the late 6th century BCE under King Yunchang, already at war with its northern neighbour, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wu">State of Wu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Defeated by Wu in 494 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">King Goujian</a> served Fuchai as a near-hostage, then rebuilt Yue with his ministers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fan-li">Fan Li</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wen-zhong">Wen Zhong</a>, by tradition sleeping on brushwood and tasting bitter gall to keep his resolve. The Yue people tattooed their bodies, cut their hair short and fought on water; their bronze swords, like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sword-of-goujian">Sword of Goujian</a> found in 1965, were the finest of the age.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goujian">Goujian</a> destroyed Wu in 473 BCE and was briefly hegemon, moving his court north to Langya. His successors declined, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a> crushed Yue around 306 BCE. Its royal kin scattered south and founded states such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/minyue">Minyue</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dongou">Dong'ou</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-yue.jpg" alt="State of Yue" width="320" height="216"><figcaption>Image: Yug · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chinese_plain_5c._BC_with_Yue-en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>510 BCE – 306 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuaiji</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yunchang, Goujian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Yue local religion and ancestral cults</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Yue</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yue, Yuyue</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-408&amp;focus=state-of-yue" aria-label="State of Yue on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7813 1082 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Yue lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8133 1338L8115 1293M8133 1338L7909 1324"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8115 1293h0M7909 1324h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8133 1338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8133 1338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-408&amp;focus=state-of-yue">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

